{"step_index":1282,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:42:19Z","thinking":"**Evaluating Radius Constraint Relationships**\n\nObserved a pattern: maximum radius plus one equals the Roman weight. Wondering if the radius can be greater than or equal to 4 when weight equals 4; no. If k is greater than or equal to 2, the weight is greater than or equal to 4. Considering how radius plus 1 relates to a weight of 4.\n\n\n**Exploring Path Graphs and Radius**\n\nConsidering path graphs $P_n$ to understand the relationship between radius and Roman weight, the radius of $P_n$ is $\\lfloor n/2 \\rfloor$. The Roman domination number $\\gamma_R(P_n)$ equals $\\lceil \\frac{2n}{3} \\rceil$. It seems that $\\lceil \\frac{2n}{3} \\rceil \\ge \\frac{n}{2} + 1$ for $n \\ge 6$, which implies the Roman weight is greater or equal to radius plus one.\n\nEvaluating if this holds generally and how to prove that radius plus 1 is less than or equal to the Roman weight, by recalling a lemma `radius_add_one_le_romanWeight_of_unique_two`.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\nimport networkx as nx\nfrom itertools import product\n\n# Test if there is always a vertex u in V_2 with eccentricity(u) <= romanWeight(f) - 1\nfound_counterexample = False\n\nfor n in range(3, 8):\n    for G in nx.graph_atlas_g():\n        if G.number_of_nodes() != n or not nx.is_connected(G):\n            continue\n        nodes = list(G.nodes())\n        for f_vals in product([0, 1, 2], repeat=n):\n            valid = True\n            for i, v in enumerate(nodes):\n                if f_vals[i] == 0:\n                    if not any(f_vals[nodes.index(u)] == 2 for u in G.neighbors(v)):\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n            if valid:\n                w = sum(f_vals)\n                v2 = [nodes[i] for i in range(n) if f_vals[i] == 2]\n                if len(v2) >= 2:\n                    eccs = [nx.eccentricity(G, u) for u in v2]\n                    if min(eccs) > w - 1:\n                        print(f'Counterexample! n={n}, w={w}, v2={v2}, eccs={eccs}, edges={list(G.edges())}')\n                        found_counterexample = True\n                        break\n        if found_counterexample:\n            break\n    if found_counterexample:\n        break\n\nif not found_counterexample:\n    print('THEOREM TRUE: There ALWAYS exists u in V_2 with eccentricity(u) <= romanWeight(f) - 1!')\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":10000,"toolAction":"Testing V_2 eccentricity bound","toolSummary":"Check if min ecc in V_2 <= W - 1"}}]}
