{"step_index":1286,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:42:31Z","thinking":"**Proving inequality with cases**\n\nConsidering the goal of proving `radius G + 1 ≤ romanWeight f`, examining cases of the Roman dominating function `f` appears necessary. With `w = romanWeight(f)` and `n = card V`, the following relationships are known: `2 ≤ w`, `radius G + 1 ≤ n`, and if `n ≤ w`, then `radius G + 1 ≤ w`.\nAlso, if `w ≤ 2`, then by `radius_le_one_of_romanWeight_le_two`, `radius G ≤ 1`. This provides initial case separation for the proof.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/.venv/bin/python -c \"\n# A graph with radius >= 4 has diameter >= 4.\n# What is the minimum number of vertices of a graph with radius >= 4?\n# In any graph, radius <= (|V| - 1).\n# If radius >= 4, then |V| - 1 >= 4, so |V| >= 5.\n# But can |V| = 5 have radius 4?\n# In a path P_5 (0-1-2-3-4), center is 2, eccentricity is 2. Radius of P_5 is 2.\n# In a path P_7 (0-1-2-3-4-5-6), center is 3, eccentricity is 3. Radius of P_7 is 3.\n# In a path P_8 (0-1-2-3-4-5-6-7), center is 3, eccentricity is 4. Radius of P_8 is 4.\n# Can any graph on <= 7 vertices have radius >= 4?\nimport networkx as nx\nmax_rad = max(nx.radius(G) for G in nx.graph_atlas_g() if nx.is_connected(G))\nprint('Max radius for n <= 7:', max_rad)\n\"","Cwd":"/mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/handoff/promising13/task_e20263a9","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolAction":"Checking max radius for n <= 7","toolSummary":"Check max radius for n <= 7"}}]}
